I prefer to zap a junk object, because invisible hostile monsters are
generally something I don't need any more of, thankyouverymuch.
Object changes BUC and/ or enchantment: Wand of cancellation.
Object turns invisible: It is a /oInvis, and you're playing slashem
No apparent result: /oInvis and you're playing vanilla (or it was an
object of unknown BUC/enchanmtent to begin with, so don't use those!)
Object disappears completely: /oTeleport

Of course by forcing you to zap-test the wandthe ccurrent
implementation forces you to use up an extra charge on the wand to ID
it (I thoroughly resent this on /oTeleport), and the YANI proposed
would change this for wands of Invis.

That said, I like this YANI, but I'd change it so that if you have see
invisible, the writing just appears without any 'transparentness'.
That means identification would still be obfuscated, because if you
had see invisible, wands of invis would give the same result as
locking, nothing, and the other "no engrave effect" wands.

Of course, that means you now have to add "invisible writing" to the
game, which will require extra coding to handle certain special cases:
(a) invisbly engraved Elbereth
(b) loss/gain of see invisble intrinsic
(c) engraving over something you can't read (as opposed to adding text
to the end of it)
(d) probably other situations I can't think of.

Of course the prospect of a "selective" elbereth that only works on
monsters that can see invisible is an interesting one. It may well
prove useful to someone, somewhere, for some bizarre reason.
Personally, I hardly ever use /oI myself: I don't like being invisible
(and even if I did, I'd only ever need one charge), so about the only
thing I ever use these wands for is rendering floating eyes harmless.
This might serve to make the wand a little more interesting.


